<h2>India's largest cumin destination, second only to chilli among China-bound spices</h2>
<p>China takes more Indian cumin than any other country — roughly USD 141 million in 2024 — making it, alongside red chilli, one of the two anchor markets for Indian spice exports into China specifically. Demand splits between direct Chinese food-processing consumption and China's role as a re-export and trading hub sending cumin onward into other Asian markets, which is part of why volume here runs meaningfully ahead of other large cumin markets like Bangladesh or the UAE.</p>

<h2>The same GACC framework, a separate registration</h2>
<p>If you already ship red chilli to China with us, it's worth knowing cumin sits under the same Decree 248 facility-registration requirement — but registration doesn't automatically extend across every product category. Confirm cumin-specific registration status before a first cumin order, even if chilli registration is already in place.</p>

<h2>Grade follows buyer type more than in most markets</h2>
<p>Because China-bound cumin serves both direct food-processing buyers and re-export trading houses, grade requirements vary more by buyer than by any fixed market standard. Processing-scale buyers often prioritise machine-clean cumin at competitive bulk pricing; trading houses buying for resale sometimes want sortex-clean to satisfy their own downstream buyers' tighter specifications. Tell us which type of buyer you're supplying and we'll match the grade.</p>

<h2>One consistent process if you're also buying chilli</h2>
<p>Cumin and red chilli into China run through essentially the same operational framework — same ports, similar 15-22 day transit, the same GACC and China-specific testing requirements. If you're running a multi-product spice programme into China, that consistency is a genuine practical advantage: one registration and compliance conversation covers the shipping mechanics for both lines, even though product-specific registration and testing still apply separately.</p>

